        <h1>Documentation</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A condensed reference. The complete one is on GitHub: every setting and environment variable in
          <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/docs/configuration.md">docs/configuration.md</a>, the API in
          <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/docs/api.md">docs/api.md</a>, and remote-server sign-in in the
          <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/README.md">README</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="config">Configuration</h2>
        <p>All config lives in the Postgres that <code>DATABASE_URL</code> names, which must be a
          <code>postgres://</code> URL: the router refuses to start without one. The compose files run a
          <code>postgres:17</code> beside it (<code>DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS</code> bounds how long boot waits for it).
          Migrations run on boot. Manage it from the web UI, <code>/graphql</code> or the
          REST API; there are no config files to edit. The shapes below are what each row holds, written as JSON.
          If something other than the router writes the tables, have it re-read them:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $MCP_ROUTER_TOKEN" \
  http://localhost:3000/api/reload</code></pre>

        <h3 id="settings">Settings</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code>{
  "port": 3000,                 <span class="cmt">// PORT env wins over this</span>
  "authToken": "a-long-secret", <span class="cmt">// MCP_ROUTER_TOKEN env wins</span>
  "authEnabled": true,          <span class="cmt">// false = unauthenticated (trusted nets only)</span>
  "allowedOrigins": [],         <span class="cmt">// extra browser Origins allowed to reach /mcp</span>
  "idleTimeoutMs": 300000,      <span class="cmt">// default stdio idle shutdown (ms)</span>
  "sessionIdleTimeoutMs": 1800000, <span class="cmt">// reclaim an idle MCP session after this (ms)</span>
  "maxSessions": 1000           <span class="cmt">// cap on live sessions; LRU-evicted past it</span>
}</code></pre>
        <p>One row. The Settings page edits the timeouts, <code>publicUrl</code> and network discovery; the port,
          address, token and auth switch are set through the environment.</p>
        <p>The optional <code>host</code> setting (or <code>HOST</code> env) picks the bind interface. Leave it
          <strong>unset to bind all interfaces</strong> — that's what Docker and LAN exposure need, so the
          published port stays reachable. Set it to <code>127.0.0.1</code> only for a bare-metal,
          localhost-only install; inside a container that would make the mapped port refuse connections.</p>
        <p>The <code>/mcp</code> endpoints run <strong>stateful sessions</strong>: <code>initialize</code> mints an
          <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code> reused across the session's requests and its GET SSE stream. Idle sessions are
          reclaimed after <code>sessionIdleTimeoutMs</code>, and a browser <strong>Origin</strong> guard (loopback +
          <code>allowedOrigins</code>) fronts <code>/mcp</code> to block DNS-rebinding — it applies even when auth is
          disabled.</p>

        <h3 id="registries">Registries</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code>[
  { "name": "official", "url": "https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io" }
]</code></pre>
        <p>Any MCP-registry-API-compatible service (<code>GET {url}/v0/servers</code>) works. The official
          registry is seeded on first run.</p>

        <h3 id="servers">Servers</h3>
        <p>One row per installed server. The name doubles as the route segment (<code>/mcp/&lt;name&gt;</code>)
          and the install directory (<code>data/servers/&lt;name&gt;</code>). Names must match
          <code>^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*$</code> (max 64 chars). A local stdio server installed from a registry:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{
  "name": "github",
  "enabled": true,
  "source": {
    "type": "registry",
    "registry": "official",
    "serverName": "io.github.github/github-mcp-server"
  },
  "transport": {
    "type": "stdio",
    "command": "node",
    "args": ["/data/servers/github/node_modules/.bin/github-mcp-server"]
  },
  "env": { "GITHUB_TOKEN": "ghp_..." }
}</code></pre>
        <p>Use <code>"source": { "type": "npm", "package": "@scope/pkg" }</code> to install straight from npm, or
          <code>"source": { "type": "remote" }</code> with a <code>streamable-http</code> transport to proxy a
          remote server without installing anything.</p>

        <h3 id="workspaces">Workspaces</h3>
        <p>A <strong>workspace</strong> is a custom aggregate: it exposes a chosen subset of servers at its own
          <code>/mcp/w/&lt;slug&gt;</code> endpoint. The <code>slug</code> is derived from the name; each member may override the base server's <code>env</code>, <code>args</code>, or
          <code>headers</code> for this workspace only. Each member runs as an isolated downstream instance, so a
          workspace can run a server that's globally disabled — and its overrides never touch the base server.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{
  "name": "Coding assistant",
  "slug": "coding-assistant",
  "enabled": true,               <span class="cmt">// false = the /mcp/w/&lt;slug&gt; endpoint 404s</span>
  "description": "GitHub + local files for a code client.",
  "members": {
    "github": { "enabled": true },
    "filesystem": {
      "enabled": true,
      "args": ["/data/servers/filesystem", "/srv/repo"],  <span class="cmt">// replaces the base args</span>
      "env": { "READ_ONLY": "1" }                          <span class="cmt">// merged over base env</span>
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p>The effective enabled state of a member is <code>workspace.enabled &amp;&amp; member.enabled</code> — the base
          server's own <code>enabled</code> flag is ignored inside a workspace. A member must name an installed server,
          and deleting a server removes it from every workspace.</p>

        <h3 id="upgrading">Upgrading from 2.x</h3>
        <p>Older routers kept their config as JSON files under <code>DATA_DIR/config</code>. The new router needs
          a Postgres: add the <code>postgres</code> service from the compose file beside it, or use one you already
          run, and set <code>DATABASE_URL</code>. On its first boot,
          while the database is empty, the router imports them in one transaction and renames each one
          <code>*.imported</code>; after that they are never read again. The image now runs as uid 1000, so a data
          folder an older (root) image wrote needs <code>sudo chown -R 1000:1000 data</code>. To back up, use
          <code>pg_dump</code>, plus <code>DATA_DIR/servers</code> for the installed npm packages.</p>

        <h2 id="api">REST API</h2>
        <p>Management API under <code>/api</code>, bearer auth, JSON in/out. Errors are non-2xx with
          <code>{ "error": "...", "detail": "..." }</code>.</p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Method &amp; path</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>GET /api/status</code></td><td>Router status (version, auth mode, running/total)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>GET/POST /api/registries</code></td><td>List / add registries</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>DELETE /api/registries/:name</code></td><td>Remove a registry</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>GET /api/registries/:name/servers</code></td><td>Search that registry</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>GET /api/servers</code></td><td>All installed servers with runtime state</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>POST /api/servers</code></td><td>Install (registry entry, npm package, or remote URL)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>PATCH /api/servers/:name</code></td><td>Update env / enabled / transport</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>DELETE /api/servers/:name</code></td><td>Stop, delete config, remove install dir</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>POST /api/servers/:name/restart</code></td><td>Kill and respawn (after env edits)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>GET /api/servers/:name/tools</code></td><td>Connect and list downstream tools</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>GET /api/workspaces</code></td><td>All workspaces with their endpoint paths</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>POST /api/workspaces</code></td><td>Create a workspace (slug auto-derived from the name)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>PATCH /api/workspaces/:slug</code></td><td>Update members / overrides / enabled (renaming re-slugs)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>DELETE /api/workspaces/:slug</code></td><td>Remove a workspace (servers untouched)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>POST /api/reload</code></td><td>Re-read config from the database and reconcile processes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="mcp">MCP endpoints</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>POST/GET/DELETE /mcp/&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>Proxy 1:1 to that server</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>POST/GET/DELETE /mcp</code></td><td>Aggregate of all enabled servers, <code>&lt;server&gt;__</code> prefix</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>POST/GET/DELETE /mcp/w/&lt;slug&gt;</code></td><td>A workspace's custom aggregate — same <code>&lt;server&gt;__</code> prefix</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Both speak streamable HTTP and require the bearer token (unless <code>authEnabled</code> is false).
          Disabled servers return 404; auth failures return 401 before any MCP handling.</p>

        <h2 id="security">Security notes</h2>
        <ul class="notes">
          <li><strong>Secrets are plaintext</strong> in the database (env values, remote headers, OAuth tokens,
            the auth token). Anyone who can read the Postgres database, its volume or a dump of it can read them.
            Protect it, and its backups.</li>
          <li><strong>One bearer token guards everything.</strong> Treat it like a password. Set it via
            <code>MCP_ROUTER_TOKEN</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Don't expose the router directly to the internet</strong> — it speaks plain HTTP. Put it
            behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy, or bind to localhost / a private network.</li>
          <li><strong>Installed servers run as child processes</strong> with the env you configure. Installing a
            server means running its code with those secrets — install packages you trust.</li>
        </ul>

